    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. id a mechanic looking at it, is there any specific way they check to tell if it's the belt or the tensioner? like is there a certain sign that it's definitely the tensioner acting up instead of just a worn belt?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A mechanic often spins the pulleys by hand while you're present to see if the tensioner rotates smoothly or if it has excessive play, which would indicate a bad internal bearing. They might also lift the belt gently to feel for worn grooves on the ribs or check the rain check chain length of the tensioner spring for fatigue. Based on these physical signs—like a loose pulley body versus a worn belt coating—they can usually distinguish between a worn, stretched belt and a faulty tensioner before even replacing parts.",
